      <species id="mwda3c0000_0002_4000_a000_da39c0000002" name="C_dmg" compartment="mw026862eb_87b1_405a_b373_64e65f51ca39" initialConcentration="0" substanceUnits="MWUSERUNIT_cell" hasOnlySubstanceUnits="true" boundaryCondition="false" constant="false">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Accumulated chemotherapy-induced cell damage in the tumour, a SLOW state standing in for therapy-induced senescence (a dedicated senescent cancer compartment is the intended successor). Dimensionless once divided by `cell`. It exists because CA19-9 secretion suppression was driven by E_chemo, which is spiky - near 1 for hours after each bolus and ~0 between doses - so its time-average barely moves across the prior and k_sec_suppress measured dead (rho 0.014). Damage integrates that exposure, giving the suppression a duty cycle long enough to survive averaging. Biological warrant: therapy-induced senescence is well documented for gemcitabine and taxanes, and CA19-9 is sialyl-Lewis-a, a differentiation antigen, so damaged/dedifferentiated cells shedding less is the expected direction.</body>
        </notes>
      </species>
